WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.680
INTRO:
When the pandemic began&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:01.680 --> 00:00:07.200
and stocks plunged, most people likely expected&nbsp;
that the housing market was next in line. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.200 --> 00:00:11.120
with the fed lowering interest rates and&nbsp;
printing loads of money, we’ve seen the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.120 --> 00:00:17.680
exact opposite unfold. The median sale price of&nbsp;
a house in the US has skyrocketed from $322,000&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.680 --> 00:00:24.800
to $428,000 within a matter of 2 years. This&nbsp;
translates to an annual growth rate of 13.2%&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.360 --> 00:00:31.120
which is literally higher than the S&amp;P 500.&nbsp;
This is of course unsustainable and it has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.120 --> 00:00:36.160
caused many to speculate that a housing crash&nbsp;
like we’ve never seen is just around the corner.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.160 --> 00:00:40.560
You just have to make one search on YouTube&nbsp;
to see how prevalent this theory really is.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.560 --> 00:00:46.000
And its popularity isn’t that surprising. The fed&nbsp;
is finally starting to raise interest rates and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:50.320
the housing market is finally starting to show&nbsp;
the first signs of a potential slowdown. There&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.320 --> 00:00:55.600
aren’t as many bids on each property and inventory&nbsp;
is slowly starting to build up. Not to mention,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.600 --> 00:01:00.000
the stock market is teetering on bear market&nbsp;
territory, so it only makes sense that this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:05.600
pain extends to real estate right? Well, yes and&nbsp;
no. While it is likely that we see a cool down in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.600 --> 00:01:11.920
real estate of 10-15% across the board and maybe&nbsp;
20-25% in some more heated areas, it’s not likely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.920 --> 00:01:16.880
that we see a 2008 style 40 to 50% collapse&nbsp;
with loads of foreclosures and here’s why.

00:01:16.880 --> 00:01:21.440
INFLATIONARY TIMES:&nbsp;

00:01:21.440 --> 00:01:26.160
Starting off, the first thing to understnad is&nbsp;
that a recession and a stock market crash are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.160 --> 00:01:31.120
not indicative of a housing market crash. In fact,&nbsp;
there’s plenty of recessions during which housing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.120 --> 00:01:36.000
continued to go up even during a recession, and&nbsp;
this is especially true during periods of high&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.000 --> 00:01:41.120
inflation. Likely the best comparison that we&nbsp;
have to today’s market environment is the 1970s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.120 --> 00:01:46.640
when there was also sky high inflation. We saw&nbsp;
inflation peak above 10% on multiple occasions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.640 --> 00:01:51.280
and it wasn’t till interest rates were raised&nbsp;
to over 10% that we finally saw inflation cool&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.280 --> 00:01:54.640
down. On top of this, there were multiple&nbsp;
recessions during this time period that lasted for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.640 --> 00:01:59.920
multiple quarters each. For example, let’s take a&nbsp;
look at the recession that took place between 1974&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.920 --> 00:02:07.600
and 1975. During this time period, the S&amp;P 500&nbsp;
got obliterated dropping a full 50%. But what do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.600 --> 00:02:12.320
you think happened to housing? Given this massive&nbsp;
sell off in the stock market, you would atleast&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.320 --> 00:02:18.160
expect a cool down in real estate right? Well, the&nbsp;
median sale price actually increased from $34,000&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.160 --> 00:02:24.880
to $39,000. In other words, housing grew by 14.7%&nbsp;
while the country experienced a recession and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.880 --> 00:02:31.360
S&amp;P 500 dropped by 50%. And this isn’t a one off&nbsp;
thing either. This happened on multiple occasions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.360 --> 00:02:36.400
during this time period. For instance, we saw&nbsp;
another recession during the first half of 1980.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.400 --> 00:02:39.840
This recession was accompanied by&nbsp;
a 20% sell off in the stock market,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.840 --> 00:02:46.080
but housing prices still went up. Similarly,&nbsp;
in the 1981 to 1982 recession, the stock market&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.080 --> 00:02:52.000
sold off 25%. This time, the housing market&nbsp;
actually had a slight pull back coming in 5.7%,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.880 --> 00:02:57.280
but nonetheless, housing prices were still&nbsp;
higher at the end of the recession than at the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.280 --> 00:03:01.920
beginning of the recession. As you can see, just&nbsp;
because there’s a recession and the stock market&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.920 --> 00:03:07.200
crashes doesn’t mean that the housing market must&nbsp;
crash as well. Something else to note is that a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.200 --> 00:03:11.520
lot of people think that the rate of increase in&nbsp;
housing prices are indicative of a housing crash,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:11.520 --> 00:03:17.680
but this is not really true either. Throughout the&nbsp;
entire decade of the 1970s, we saw abnormally high&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.680 --> 00:03:22.240
growth in real estate prices. When President&nbsp;
Nixon dropped the gold standard in 1971,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.240 --> 00:03:28.480
the median sale price stood at $25,800. 10&nbsp;
years later, the median sale price stood&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.480 --> 00:03:34.800
at $69,400. This means that housing prices grew&nbsp;
168% during these 10 years. That translates to an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.800 --> 00:03:40.800
annual return of 10.4% for an entire decade. While&nbsp;
that might be hard to believe at first glance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.800 --> 00:03:44.720
it makes perfect sense when you account&nbsp;
for inflation. The average inflation rate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.720 --> 00:03:51.680
during these 10 years was a whopping 8.42%, so&nbsp;
really, real estate only grew about 2% per year&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.680 --> 00:03:56.240
this during time period which is very sustainable.&nbsp;
The thing to keep in mind is that real estate is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.240 --> 00:04:01.040
one of the most trusted and reliable safe havens&nbsp;
to protect against inflation. So, it only makes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.040 --> 00:04:05.200
sense that real estate would at the very least&nbsp;
keep pace with inflation regardless of whether&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.200 --> 00:04:10.160
there’s a recession or a stock market crash. So,&nbsp;
don’t think that housing prices have to come down&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.160 --> 00:04:15.360
just because they’ve gone up so fast. As long as&nbsp;
inflation is high, that scenario remains unlikely.

00:04:15.360 --> 00:04:19.520
RISING MORTGAGE RATES:&nbsp;

00:04:19.520 --> 00:04:23.520
Another popular argument for a housing&nbsp;
market crash is rising interest rates.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.520 --> 00:04:28.160
People argue that as interest rates go up, people&nbsp;
will be able afford less meaning that sellers will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.160 --> 00:04:33.200
have to decrease prices. While this argument makes&nbsp;
sense during normal periods, the thing to consider&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.200 --> 00:04:39.200
is that supply is extraordinarily low. Housing&nbsp;
inventory is literally at 40 year lows and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.200 --> 00:04:44.000
trend has only been downwards. So, it’s not&nbsp;
clear how much rising interest rates will even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.000 --> 00:04:48.640
affect housing prices. For example, let’s say&nbsp;
that 30 year mortgage rates go all the way to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.640 --> 00:04:54.800
8% from their lows of about 3%. The general rule&nbsp;
is that for every 1% increase in mortgage rates,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.800 --> 00:04:59.760
purchasing power decreases by roughly 10%.&nbsp;
So, a 5% increase in mortgage rates would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.760 --> 00:05:05.520
decrease purchasing power by 50%. Now, that’s&nbsp;
quite a decrease, but even if this results in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.520 --> 00:05:10.560
inventory doubling, we’d only be back to pre&nbsp;
pandemic levels. In other words, the decrease in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.560 --> 00:05:15.920
purchasing power would more or less just wipe out&nbsp;
the excess demand. So, while houses may no longer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.920 --> 00:05:20.320
be selling for crazy amounts above listing, I’m&nbsp;
not sure this would drag down the listing prices&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.320 --> 00:05:25.120
themselves all that much. Something else to&nbsp;
note is that even with these massive hikes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.120 --> 00:05:30.320
interest rates are still super low on a historical&nbsp;
basis. And even more importantly, real mortgage&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.320 --> 00:05:35.440
rates are still negative. While a 6% mortgage&nbsp;
rate may sound high compared to 12 months ago,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.440 --> 00:05:40.960
6% is still lower than the 8% inflation that we’ve&nbsp;
been seeing. So, from an investment perspective,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.960 --> 00:05:45.120
taking out a mortgage right now is still&nbsp;
rather appealing. And the last thing to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.120 --> 00:05:49.040
consider regarding mortgage rates is that they’re&nbsp;
completely irrelevant to a significant amount of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.040 --> 00:05:53.920
buyers because they’re buying with cash. Now,&nbsp;
this isn’t to say that they don’t keep tabs on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.920 --> 00:05:59.120
mortgage rates, but in terms of purchasing power,&nbsp;
it doesn’t affect them whatsoever. I’m sure these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.120 --> 00:06:02.800
guys would like for the real estate market to&nbsp;
cool off as well so that they can buy lower.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.800 --> 00:06:07.280
But, most of them aren’t going to wait for a crash&nbsp;
because they have excess cash reserves that need&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:07.280 --> 00:06:12.640
protection from inflation. I mean these 13&nbsp;
companies alone hold a trillion in cash.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.200 --> 00:06:18.000
Now, Apple is likely not going to go out and pick&nbsp;
up single family homes, but banks and financial&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.000 --> 00:06:23.360
institutions will and that’s exactly what&nbsp;
they’ve been doing. In q4 of 2021, for example,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.360 --> 00:06:28.640
almost 20% all US home sales were to institutional&nbsp;
investors who were more than likely paying cash.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.640 --> 00:06:32.240
So, rising mortgage rates probably&nbsp;
won’t cause a housing market collapse,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.240 --> 00:06:37.120
and if you don’t believe me, just take a look&nbsp;
at the 1980s. After a decade of high inflation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.120 --> 00:06:41.760
in the 1970s, the fed decided to jack up&nbsp;
interest rates to unprecedented amounts.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:41.760 --> 00:06:47.680
This caused 30 year mortgage rates to nearly reach&nbsp;
20%. Given that mortgage rates were at 8.5% just a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:47.680 --> 00:06:52.720
few years before this, this means that mortgage&nbsp;
rates rose 10%. According to our rule of thumb,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.720 --> 00:06:57.440
purchasing power should have decreased 100%,&nbsp;
and housing prices should have gotten flamed.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.440 --> 00:07:03.600
But in reality, real estate prices actually ended&nbsp;
up rallying 100% over the next decade. So, if an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.600 --> 00:07:08.960
18% mortgage rate didn’t cause a crash, there’s&nbsp;
no gaurantee that a 7 to 8% mortgage rate will.

00:07:08.960 --> 00:07:13.200
NO FUNDAMENTAL CRISIS:&nbsp;

00:07:13.200 --> 00:07:17.440
Yet another factor that is preventing a housing&nbsp;
crash from taking place is that home buyers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:17.440 --> 00:07:22.640
are more qualified than ever before. Here’s the&nbsp;
thing, for a 2008 style collapse to take place,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:22.640 --> 00:07:27.280
there needs to be a fundamental crisis. Secular&nbsp;
variations in supply and demand due to mortgage&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:27.280 --> 00:07:31.920
rates and inflation aren’t going to crash&nbsp;
the housing market 40% within 12 months.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:31.920 --> 00:07:35.600
For that, you need a fundamental crisis like&nbsp;
loads of unqualified buyers or threateningly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:35.600 --> 00:07:39.600
low cash reserves across financial&nbsp;
instutions. But, we’re not seeing that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:39.600 --> 00:07:44.240
we’re actually seeing the exact opposite.&nbsp;
The average homebuyer is more qualified&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:44.240 --> 00:07:49.280
than ever, and the reason for this is that the&nbsp;
average person can no longer afford these homes.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:49.280 --> 00:07:54.160
So, it’s primarily just high income families&nbsp;
that are buying homes. In Texas, for example,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:54.160 --> 00:07:59.280
the median homebuyer has a household income of&nbsp;
$112,000. This puts the average homebuyer in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:59.280 --> 00:08:04.560
Texas in the 71th percentile in terms of household&nbsp;
income. In other words, the majority of homes are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.560 --> 00:08:09.200
being sold to people who are well off. So, it’s&nbsp;
quite unlikely that we’re gonna see a crisis were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.200 --> 00:08:13.760
people suddenly can’t pay their mortgages. Now,&nbsp;
you might be saying that these people may lose&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:13.760 --> 00:08:18.320
their jobs during a recession which could change&nbsp;
their financial position. But, the truth is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:18.320 --> 00:08:22.698
the vast majority of job losses are usually&nbsp;
amongst everyday blue collar workers. These white&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:22.698 --> 00:08:27.040
collar workers are generally some of the last&nbsp;
to lose their jobs because of two reasons.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:27.040 --> 00:08:31.360
One, these employees are harder to replace so&nbsp;
companies are more reluctant to let them go.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.920 --> 00:08:36.240
And two, most of these guys work at cash rich IT&nbsp;
companies that can afford to weather a recession.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:36.800 --> 00:08:40.880
Also, even if the company can’t weather the&nbsp;
recession, it’s more than likely that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:40.880 --> 00:08:45.680
homebuyer can weather it themselves. The median&nbsp;
bank account balance rises exponentially with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:45.680 --> 00:08:51.600
income. For example, the median bank account&nbsp;
balance in the US is only $5,300. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:51.600 --> 00:08:56.960
the median bank account balance of someone earning&nbsp;
between $90 to $100,000 is $70,000. The median&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:56.960 --> 00:09:01.600
homebuyer in Texas actually makes even more&nbsp;
than this, so it’s likely that they have nearly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:01.600 --> 00:09:08.880
$100,000 in savings. The median monthly payment&nbsp;
on a new mortgage was $1,931 in February. Even if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:08.880 --> 00:09:13.600
we say that it’s $2,500, these median homebuyers&nbsp;
could keep up their mortgage for two full years&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:13.600 --> 00:09:18.640
before running into problems. Also, it’s not&nbsp;
just homebuyers who are on top of their finances.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:18.640 --> 00:09:24.880
Bank are on top of their finances as well. JP&nbsp;
Morgan Chase for example is hold $1.5 trillion in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:24.880 --> 00:09:31.520
cash and Bank of America has $895 billion in cash.&nbsp;
Clearly, these institutions are more liquid than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:31.520 --> 00:09:36.560
ever before meaning that the odds of a fundamental&nbsp;
crisis are also lower than ever before.

00:09:36.560 --> 00:09:40.960
THE TRUTH ABOUT A HOUSING COLLAPSE:&nbsp;

00:09:40.960 --> 00:09:45.600
In the end, there’s no question that housing is&nbsp;
becoming increasingly unaffordable for the average&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:45.600 --> 00:09:51.360
person. But, this doesn’t mean that housing market&nbsp;
is going to collapse anytime soon. It appears that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:51.360 --> 00:09:55.680
many people are hoping for a massive plummet so&nbsp;
that they can finally buy for a reasonable price,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:55.680 --> 00:10:01.120
but the truth is that 2008 was a one off thing.&nbsp;
I mean, just take a look at the last 60 years&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:01.120 --> 00:10:05.760
worth of pricing data. There are times were&nbsp;
the market goes sideways or cools off 10-15%,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:06.560 --> 00:10:10.560
but 2008 was the only time that the&nbsp;
housing market actually crashed.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:10.560 --> 00:10:15.280
Since then, regulation has tightened significantly&nbsp;
and lenders have become extremely cautious.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:15.280 --> 00:10:19.840
So, it’s primarily just high income families and&nbsp;
cash buyers who are driving the current market.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:19.840 --> 00:10:24.960
On top of this, from the 1970s, we’ve seen that&nbsp;
real estate can continue skyrocketing despite&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:24.960 --> 00:10:30.080
recessions, stock market crashes, and interest&nbsp;
rate hikes. Now, this isn’t to say that we won’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:30.080 --> 00:10:34.800
see a cool off like we’ve already been seeing.&nbsp;
As mortgage rates rise and inventory builds up,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:34.800 --> 00:10:39.200
the real estate market will no doubt soften.&nbsp;
But, to correlate that to a full on housing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:39.200 --> 00:10:44.400
crash is quite a leap in logic. To be honest,&nbsp;
the vast majority of articles and videos that are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:44.400 --> 00:10:48.320
telling people that there’s gonna be a monumental&nbsp;
housing crash are just preying on these people’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:48.320 --> 00:10:54.080
desperateness to afford a house. But, that’s just&nbsp;
what I think, and I’m just some random guy on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:54.080 --> 00:10:56.960
internet and I could be completely wrong. Do you&nbsp;
guys think there’s going to be a housing collapse?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:56.960 --> 00:11:02.160
Comment that down below. Also, drop a like if&nbsp;
you hope that inflation doesn’t get much worse.&nbsp;&nbsp;

